#Task 2: Git Basic Workflow & Undo Operations
Situation
You are working on a project and accidentally make mistakes while committing code.
Tasks:
Initialize a Git repository.
Add README.md and commit it.
Modify a file and check changes before committing.
Unstage a file after adding it.
Fix a wrong commit message.
Revert a commit safely.

1. Initialize a Git Repository
git init
Initializes an empty Git repository in the current folder.
2. Add README.md and Commit It
touch README.md
git add README.md
git commit -m "Initial commit with README"

Adds README.md to staging and commits it.
3. Modify a File and Check Changes Before Committing
echo "Project description" >> README.md
git status
git diff
git status shows modified files
git diff shows exact changes before commit
4. Unstage a File After Adding It
git add README.md
git restore --staged README.md
Removes the file from staging without deleting changes.
git reset README.md

5. Fix a Wrong Commit Message
git commit --amend -m "Correct commit message"

Updates the last commit message without creating a new commit.
6. Revert a Commit Safely
git log --oneline
git revert <commit-hash>
Creates a new commit that safely undoes the changes of the selected commit.

Conclusion
Git provides powerful undo commands such as restore, amend, and revert to fix mistakes without losing work, ensuring safe and controlled version management.


#Task 3: Branching, Merging & Conflict Resolution
Situation
Two developers are working on the same file and a merge conflict occurs.
Tasks:
Create a branch feature-ui.
Make changes and commit.
Merge the branch into main.
Simulate a merge conflict.
Resolve the conflict manually and complete the merge.
Explain why merge conflicts occur

1. Create a Branch feature-ui
git branch feature-ui
git checkout feature-ui
git checkout -b feature-ui
Creates and switches to the feature-ui branch.

2. Make Changes and Commit
echo "UI feature added" >> app.txt
git add app.txt
git commit -m "Added UI feature"
Changes are saved in the feature-ui branch.

3. Merge the Branch into main
git checkout main
git merge feature-ui
If no conflict exists, Git merges successfully.

4. Simulate a Merge Conflict
Step 1: Modify the Same File in main
echo "Main branch update" >> app.txt
git add app.txt
git commit -m "Updated app in main branch"

Step 2: Modify the Same Line in feature-ui
git checkout feature-ui
echo "Conflicting UI change" >> app.txt
git add app.txt
git commit -m "UI conflicting change"

Step 3: Try to Merge
git checkout main
git merge feature-ui


Git reports a merge conflict.

5. Resolve the Conflict Manually

Open app.txt:

<<<<<<< HEAD
Main branch update
=======
Conflicting UI change
>>>>>>> feature-ui

Edit the File Manually
Main branch update
Conflicting UI change

Mark Conflict as Resolved
git add app.txt
git commit -m "Resolved merge conflict between main and feature-ui"
Merge completed successfully.

6. Why Do Merge Conflicts Occur?
Merge conflicts occur when:
Two branches modify the same line of a file
One branch deletes a file while another modifies it
Git cannot decide which change to keep automatically
Git stops the merge process and asks the developer to manually resolve the conflict to avoid losing important changes.

Conclusion
Branching allows parallel development, while merging combines work. Merge conflicts are a natural part of collaborative development and can be resolved safely by manually reviewing and editing conflicting files.



